    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1160">

                <pb facs="1175" n="1009" sameAs="frus:frus1917/pg1009"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.516/164</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        Ambassador Fletcher
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 40</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-03-19">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 19, 1917</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the Department’s telegram No.
                    654, of February 6, 1917, addressed to Mr. Parker, directing him to lodge a
                    protest with the <hi rendition="#italic">de facto</hi> Government of Mexico
                    against its apparent purpose to force into liquidation the National Bank of
                    Mexico and the Bank of London and Mexico, there is enclosed herewith, for your
                    information, a translation of a note, dated March 13, 1917, from the French
                    Ambassador at this Capital, from which it appears that further forced loans have
                    been exacted from the National Bank of Mexico.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
